rnative C3 convertase includes C3b."},"references":[],"review":"new_independent_provisional_answer_high_specificity_fact_85","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0744cc8a6f6cf2f06d6ec3a131036219ad5535490f9f543867424e5341383c99"},"q-204f7020071f":{"correct":"C","answerText":"stomach","explanation":"Gasterophilus larvae attach to the stomach lining of horses and mature in the gastrointestinal tract.","optionReasons":{"A":"This choice does not fit the key distinction in the question. Gasterophilus larvae attach to the stomach lining of horses and mature in the gastrointestinal tract.","B":"This choice does not fit the key distinction in the question. Gasterophilus larvae attach to the stomach lining of horses and mature in the gastrointestinal tract.","C":"Gasterophilus larvae attach to the stomach lining of horses and mature in the gastrointestinal tract.","D":"This choice does not fit the key distinction in the question. Gasterophilus larvae attach to the stomach lining of horses and mature in the gastrointestinal tract.","E":"This choice does not fit the key distinction in the question. Gasterophilus larvae attach to the stomach lining of horses and mature in the gastrointestinal tract."},"references":[],"review":"new_independent_provisional_answer_high_specificity_fact_58","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"cc8233b24be8a7e38973ee33f807a405704b4583b2478a500a65f0c833380e1e"},"q-1dd63084140f":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Ciprofloxacin","explanation":"Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and can raise theophylline concentrations.","optionReasons":{"A":"This choice does not fit the key distinction in the question. Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and can raise theophylline concentrations.","B":"Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and can raise theophylline concentrations.","C":"This choice does not fit the key distinction in the question. Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and can raise theophylline concentrations.","D":"This choice does not fit the key distinction in the question. Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and can raise theophylline concentrations."},"references":[],"review":"new_independent_provisional_answer_high_specificity_fact_79","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0acdae2b3a18681f9d7e6814417c737255b798d78387e0cb6a5f1ed017277dd1"},"q-98b2e25bab27":{"correct":"B","answerText":"It must be lipid soluble","explanation":"To cross intact skin for systemic delivery, a drug usually needs adequate lipid solubility; other properties such as potency and molecular size also matter.","optionReasons":{"B":"To cross intact skin for systemic delivery, a drug usually needs adequate lipid solubility; other properties such as potency and molecular size also matter."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"97deb47702407ec7ad2ef884dcab5d20c9c89cbc2727a3325ed9541409e0ec09"},"q-e2526b43c71e":{"correct":"C","answerText":"is applied to the epidermis","explanation":"A transdermal formulation is placed on the skin and delivers drug across the epidermal barrier; injections and rectal administration use different routes.","optionReasons":{"C":"A transdermal formulation is placed on the skin and delivers drug across the epidermal barrier; injections and rectal administration use different routes."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"fc903ff08cce6aa4d6376863480625d5e493ffb852a485ecbe5861233003b57c"},"q-3b4412630f34":{"correct":"A","answerText":"the posterior pituitary to release more ADH","explanation":"Raised plasma osmolality stimulates hypothalamic osmoreceptors, increasing posterior-pituitary vasopressin (ADH) release to conserve water.","optionReasons":{"A":"Raised plasma osmolality stimulates hypothalamic osmoreceptors, increasing posterior-pituitary vasopressin (ADH) release to conserve water."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"719c294ec9602bc74d95f722d6b5cbbe0f683e69a845ba9cd64f3aaf68c8f152"},"q-6c34c3e832d2":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Hydrophilic substances","explanation":"Hydrophilic charged molecules generally cross lipid membranes poorly without channels or carriers, whereas small lipophilic uncharged molecules diffuse more readily.","optionReasons":{"D":"Hydrophilic charged molecules generally cross lipid membranes poorly without channels or carriers, whereas small lipophilic uncharged molecules diffuse more readily."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"36527ba1ddfd9b048d8753144f90319ad3cbcf218ece340a18eac1cb6cf4d45c"},"q-49d610a17e66":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Low ability to penetrate through the cell membrane lipids","explanation":"Hydrophilic compounds have low passive permeability through lipid bilayers, limiting penetration into cells and across the blood–brain barrier.","optionReasons":{"A":"Hydrophilic compounds have low passive permeability through lipid bilayers, limiting penetration into cells and across the blood–brain barrier."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"10e14574b7ea7ef125af69c56878415824c31469aff54c0519be83d7d83e5e89"},"q-83a86a15a034":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Rectal","explanation":"Rectal administration can partly bypass the portal circulation; oral and intraduodenal absorption ordinarily passes through the liver before systemic distribution.","optionReasons":{"C":"Rectal administration can partly bypass the portal circulation; oral and intraduodenal absorption ordinarily passes through the liver before systemic distribution."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0f984d46ee2f515a91c5805e4529582a95a1da8b8d61e476af32669accb640f5"},"q-999eeb670cb8":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Absorption depends on GI tract secretion and motor function","explanation":"Oral absorption is influenced by gastric emptying, intestinal motility, GI secretions and drug dissolution; first-pass metabolism may occur.","optionReasons":{"B":"Oral absorption is influenced by gastric emptying, intestinal motility, GI secretions and drug dissolution; first-pass metabolism may occur."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"f232af2bbf8e1daadb8609c7188df64e6ef0010273f4fdd7cadb5d29943f41a3"},"q-f7e28bfbd031":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Pretty fast absorption","explanation":"Highly vascular sublingual mucosa allows some drugs to enter systemic circulation rapidly while reducing first-pass hepatic metabolism.","optionReasons":{"A":"Highly vascular sublingual mucosa allows some drugs to enter systemic circulation rapidly while reducing first-pass hepatic metabolism."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"91c26a67a85e12070925e548b1f8fe4c792d536c5ea2a6774741d7e772d41552"},"q-8ddc506d977c":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Inhalation provides slow access to the general circulation","explanation":"Inhaled drugs can be absorbed rapidly through the large alveolar surface; therefore the claim that inhalation necessarily provides slow systemic access is false.","optionReasons":{"C":"Inhaled drugs can be absorbed rapidly through the large alveolar surface; therefore the claim that inhalation necessarily provides slow systemic access is false."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3a90c809c3f95556a356e027d1b8444bbce3553569a02eb67271cc68c95bc15f"},"q-d0e46c26005e":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Absence of pores in the brain capillary endothelium","explanation":"Blood–brain barrier capillaries possess tight endothelial junctions and minimal fenestration, restricting passive entry of many polar drugs.","optionReasons":{"C":"Blood–brain barrier capillaries possess tight endothelial junctions and minimal fenestration, restricting passive entry of many polar drugs."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"7d3e6c096eef8641a41dcdd9f4d8992810b37129f1e6d3112806b7f51bb141e6"},"q-7bd8aea200b4":{"correct":"D","answerText":"The amount of a drug in the body to the concentration of a drug in plasma","explanation":"The apparent volume of distribution equals the amount of drug in the body divided by its measured plasma concentration.","optionReasons":{"D":"The apparent volume of distribution equals the amount of drug in the body divided by its measured plasma concentration."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"17a756d2ca336ed0d3f32227f93775551320631d0815c376caec83cd5f8fed04"},"q-533393075d33":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Concentration of a substance in plasma","explanation":"Plasma drug concentration is needed to compute volume of distribution from the amount of drug present in the body.","optionReasons":{"A":"Plasma drug concentration is needed to compute volume of distribution from the amount of drug present in the body."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"be21df88aec8a4967ec31a93a35035fba969f65fc40e35b2ebf24969fdd4ae5e"},"q-bba7c80fe79a":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Process of physicochemical and biochemical alteration of a drug in the body","explanation":"Drug biotransformation means enzymatic or other chemical modification of a drug, mainly in the liver, rather than storage or plasma binding.","optionReasons":{"D":"Drug biotransformation means enzymatic or other chemical modification of a drug, mainly in the liver, rather than storage or plasma binding."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"754c790f6c0799813860ff567cd3404cf8666644b1ccf78097dcd5ddc4d4b6a9"},"q-2ae8cf880ad3":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Less lipid soluble","explanation":"Drug metabolism commonly increases polarity and reduces lipid solubility, facilitating excretion; active metabolites can be exceptions.","optionReasons":{"D":"Drug metabolism commonly increases polarity and reduces lipid solubility, facilitating excretion; active metabolites can be exceptions."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c3fd7b52449ac37c7653e96174dd240772b54a6bfea70b860c90c3016c451226"},"q-bbef4caa8a51":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Lipid soluble","explanation":"Hepatic microsomal oxidative enzymes commonly metabolize lipophilic drugs to make them more polar and easier to eliminate.","optionReasons":{"A":"Hepatic microsomal oxidative enzymes commonly metabolize lipophilic drugs to make them more polar and easier to eliminate."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3d929a46588496330da7cae666de7e154ac7b8ca590f0707d39d7de0fcba8486"},"q-a3eb81c9abe4":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Faster urinary excretion","explanation":"Metabolism usually generates more polar drug products and thus promotes renal or biliary excretion, although some prodrugs require activation.","optionReasons":{"A":"Metabolism usually generates more polar drug products and thus promotes renal or biliary excretion, although some prodrugs require activation."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3496f9ad3f49b2dc689c8a0ce0baf47a44e1faa9fe83e49f03491b6eb615343a"},"q-cfba41cd94ed":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Acetylation","explanation":"Acetylation is a phase II conjugation reaction, whereas oxidation, reduction and hydrolysis are classic phase I reactions.","optionReasons":{"A":"Acetylation is a phase II conjugation reaction, whereas oxidation, reduction and hydrolysis are classic phase I reactions."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"9fe3a4b90eecb7d94695e225487e43ff84f4275dd51997793ef1ec3fd2474b57"},"q-1fba711ea69f":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Enlarged","explanation":"Reduced hepatic microsomal metabolism can slow elimination of susceptible drugs, extending their duration of action.","optionReasons":{"B":"Reduced hepatic microsomal metabolism can slow elimination of susceptible drugs, extending their duration of action."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"25289d4da2d29810e0e2f0102925881cb617e4d9c3f4af3dfda275d6826a76a2"},"q-a3be936edd1f":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Change the amount of a drug in plasma by half during elimination","explanation":"Elimination half-life is the time for plasma drug amount or concentration to fall by half during first-order elimination.","optionReasons":{"A":"Elimination half-life is the time for plasma drug amount or concentration to fall by half during first-order elimination."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"90da48608bbf3187c146f1530c38709e90e5f8437034036fb59b834e38477763"},"q-9f9f06f97628":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Mechanisms of drug action","explanation":"Pharmacodynamics asks what drugs do to the body, including receptor interactions and mechanisms of action; absorption and elimination are pharmacokinetics.","optionReasons":{"A":"Pharmacodynamics asks what drugs do to the body, including receptor interactions and mechanisms of action; absorption and elimination are pharmacokinetics."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a3c4d4bf2d4e10307fdbaca8491e8b21bcac5572ef4c67e7daf9a83790200c9b"},"q-acd556e13721":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Information about unwanted effects","explanation":"Adverse effects are among the pharmacodynamic effects of a drug; absorption, distribution and excretion are pharmacokinetic topics.","optionReasons":{"B":"Adverse effects are among the pharmacodynamic effects of a drug; absorption, distribution and excretion are pharmacokinetic topics."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"9269029957e21b1eb1e1c4ba8c5aefc0afb6960c8c31b477f4cf2be0c06d75cb"},"q-f53d487f2a39":{"correct":"B","answerText":"A measure of how tightly a drug binds to a receptor","explanation":"Affinity describes how strongly a ligand binds its receptor, distinct from efficacy and plasma protein binding.","optionReasons":{"B":"Affinity describes how strongly a ligand binds its receptor, distinct from efficacy and plasma protein binding."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"d9f8e5c1f59b195a10754587248bfa058f80dd3f7bca0a00024225a867f0b3d1"},"q-eff32d134e14":{"correct":"D","answerText":"All of the above","explanation":"Drug targets include receptors, ion channels and transporter proteins, so all three listed categories can be drug targets.","optionReasons":{"D":"Drug targets include receptors, ion channels and transporter proteins, so all three listed categories can be drug targets."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a5594cfe789a4dc6cba7e52e3bb6bf2daa8ec44f59ce1ea7254d5c26da45c726"},"q-517467a8e120":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Interacts with the receptor and initiates changes in cell function, producing various effects","explanation":"A receptor agonist binds a receptor and activates signaling that changes cellular function; binding without activation characterizes an antagonist.","optionReasons":{"B":"A receptor agonist binds a receptor and activates signaling that changes cellular function; binding without activation characterizes an antagonist."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"cb02c01abf4f8b28e5ef2d40f40429477015b9b1d9a1f6db79116e8a0b650b12"},"q-715246756b97":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Full agonist","explanation":"A full agonist can elicit the maximal achievable response in an appropriate system; partial agonists have lower intrinsic efficacy.","optionReasons":{"D":"A full agonist can elicit the maximal achievable response in an appropriate system; partial agonists have lower intrinsic efficacy."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"284a0582378598d311f4b837b02b919964c23906543f014a823cc3ca6994c063"},"q-4ebd4f709785":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Partial agonist","explanation":"A partial agonist activates the receptor but has a lower maximal efficacy than a full agonist even when receptors are occupied.","optionReasons":{"A":"A partial agonist activates the receptor but has a lower maximal efficacy than a full agonist even when receptors are occupied."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"6cb0b516a391d5e3cdc0eeb2a800b4f363cafcac7e2ea8e89631f9f738dcd306"},"q-7dd75778404e":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Binds to the receptors without directly altering their functions","explanation":"An antagonist occupies a receptor without activating it and prevents an agonist from producing its normal effect.","optionReasons":{"D":"An antagonist occupies a receptor without activating it and prevents an agonist from producing its normal effect."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3863b8844b7b5e082f6e9d6a452975fc7a55b7e7902fc7016b949fd4b103384a"},"q-e61ff8b020a0":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Binds to the same receptor site and progressively inhibits the agonist response","explanation":"A competitive antagonist typically binds at the agonist binding site and shifts the agonist concentration–response curve rightward.","optionReasons":{"B":"A competitive antagonist typically binds at the agonist binding site and shifts the agonist concentration–response curve rightward."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"d4e063f44338f235b27cb47efd34a57b851b07af6284e018a2db4418f0978965"},"q-ccb0f3370dbe":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Covalent bonds","explanation":"Covalent binding can make receptor antagonism effectively irreversible until receptors are replaced.","optionReasons":{"C":"Covalent binding can make receptor antagonism effectively irreversible until receptors are replaced."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a08cc50a7a74445a36f08af0360b974bb5121de8a4dd23be8462a50998643d5a"},"q-f670061381eb":{"correct":"D","answerText":"cAMP","explanation":"Cyclic AMP is a classic intracellular second messenger; adenylyl cyclase is an enzyme that produces it.","optionReasons":{"D":"Cyclic AMP is a classic intracellular second messenger; adenylyl cyclase is an enzyme that produces it."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"928cd39e747cfd0f4017361b4d3c1bcc166677c5580e0e60e4deca98c885ff08"},"q-eb7d01d69277":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Proteinkinases activation and protein phosphorylation","explanation":"Several second messengers, including cAMP and Ca2+, act through protein kinases and downstream phosphorylation pathways.","optionReasons":{"B":"Several second messengers, including cAMP and Ca2+, act through protein kinases and downstream phosphorylation pathways."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"36284a3256c936764032d46113921d7aaf4c4e58e80f7ec5f67b138c18992e55"},"q-e83e497d21bf":{"correct":"D","answerText":"All of the above","explanation":"Sodium-channel blockers, calcium-channel blockers and potassium-channel openers all act on ion channels.","optionReasons":{"D":"Sodium-channel blockers, calcium-channel blockers and potassium-channel openers all act on ion channels."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"92914d527669db048dc041536fd8b4a5c683a0332f6555a01aa01bd67dc35a6f"},"q-a5cda7a3d74f":{"correct":"C","answerText":"The amount of a substance to produce the required effect in most patients","explanation":"A therapeutic dose is an amount expected to produce a desired clinical effect in the intended patient population.","optionReasons":{"C":"A therapeutic dose is an amount expected to produce a desired clinical effect in the intended patient population."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5204e889ee411427f37da402ed115ea5f20579d2451b438d2a166edaa442bb87"},"q-d63ee5ed3dc7":{"correct":"B","answerText":"The amount of substance to produce effects hazardous for an organism","explanation":"A toxic dose is one at which harmful effects arise rather than desired therapeutic effects.","optionReasons":{"B":"A toxic dose is one at which harmful effects arise rather than desired therapeutic effects."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c4727b03c7ac3231133db8bd007ecda42c72b6195045d9fe57288f622aebc645"},"q-f157cce6056a":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Cumulative effect","explanation":"Accumulation occurs when repeated dosing outpaces elimination, increasing concentrations and risk of toxicity.","optionReasons":{"B":"Accumulation occurs when repeated dosing outpaces elimination, increasing concentrations and risk of toxicity."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"f9debcb8dc6abb6aa2fe7ec340dacde8df8f874fc68985bf9003662800d5ff82"},"q-6139b1f167ed":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Tolerance","explanation":"Tolerance develops as reduced drug responsiveness over repeated administration, often over days to weeks.","optionReasons":{"C":"Tolerance develops as reduced drug responsiveness over repeated administration, often over days to weeks."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ce0db84feeec7458cbcdc8eb85bcb0df60e3ef35c259ad66c286eebbbe1d1aec"},"q-f547f2cace6b":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Tachyphylaxis","explanation":"Tachyphylaxis is a rapid decline in drug responsiveness over minutes or a few repeated exposures.","optionReasons":{"D":"Tachyphylaxis is a rapid decline in drug responsiveness over minutes or a few repeated exposures."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0ee06ba982e893d7b2709b7ecb54b903d2ce60aece334250266f062f93496c9b"},"q-bef0bab2cda5":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Increased metabolic degradation","explanation":"Enhanced metabolism can lower drug exposure and contribute to pharmacokinetic tolerance or apparent resistance.","optionReasons":{"B":"Enhanced metabolism can lower drug exposure and contribute to pharmacokinetic tolerance or apparent resistance."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"35ef91e4d941f02158cf6d549b488d8ac70d76e52fe1d811d9397efc5a834c87"},"q-e810c1ef4412":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Change in receptors, loss of them or exhaustion of mediators","explanation":"Downregulation or desensitization of receptors and depletion of endogenous mediators can reduce responsiveness to repeated doses.","optionReasons":{"A":"Downregulation or desensitization of receptors and depletion of endogenous mediators can reduce responsiveness to repeated doses."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a0e173da5c2745e6aef0948bfaa9a30d3440ae7c667f5b4cd9656cd3e4b1a661"},"q-f215fda577ba":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Abstinence syndrome","explanation":"Withdrawal or abstinence syndrome occurs after abrupt discontinuation of a dependence-producing substance and may include physical and psychological symptoms.","optionReasons":{"C":"Withdrawal or abstinence syndrome occurs after abrupt discontinuation of a dependence-producing substance and may include physical and psychological symptoms."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c78a31179c7e9aa8e9bd06ca67e93fadcd54c5da6a1fb7769dac41ad2ac8d527"},"q-db2810373d5c":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Pharmacokinetic interaction","explanation":"A drug interaction affecting absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion is pharmacokinetic, not pharmacodynamic.","optionReasons":{"D":"A drug interaction affecting absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion is pharmacokinetic, not pharmacodynamic."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"67706e08d2588587d7d5043409667fe86a30603a3a2ca828af829b3cb6f5eb1c"},"q-757171752d69":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Pharmacodynamic interaction","explanation":"A pharmacodynamic interaction occurs when drugs influence each other's effects at receptors, signaling pathways or physiological systems.","optionReasons":{"A":"A pharmacodynamic interaction occurs when drugs influence each other's effects at receptors, signaling pathways or physiological systems."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"81b748808fb857eff539091965914a76b4f2baa00344e141f1e939506d965cb9"},"q-95897a207522":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Additive effect","explanation":"An additive interaction is defined by a combined effect equal to the sum of the two separate effects.","optionReasons":{"C":"An additive interaction is defined by a combined effect equal to the sum of the two separate effects."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c4bede37aa8312a462e2bb6bc85a9bb9ade533fb9a0e12fafdf027ec989015bb"},"q-7d38a6e5f272":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Intensive increase of drug effects due to their combination","explanation":"Potentiation occurs when combining drugs makes an effect stronger than it would be from the individual drug effect alone.","optionReasons":{"D":"Potentiation occurs when combining drugs makes an effect stronger than it would be from the individual drug effect alone."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1e2cda27021933aa9b05e1287f1ff1c6d181c3e1d51ca15dddc80f576704fe5a"},"q-0866e8cb8e88":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Competitive","explanation":"Competitive receptor antagonism is a recognized type of antagonism; summation and potentiation describe combined effects, not antagonism.","optionReasons":{"D":"Competitive receptor antagonism is a recognized type of antagonism; summation and potentiation describe combined effects, not antagonism."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"42dc8ed98e9c3856dfc291e44f6614b4a2039d24cf7c58174a9fb88233cda771"},"q-6eee3a3d8f56":{"correct":"A","answerText":"two drugs combine with one another to form an inactive compound","explanation":"Chemical antagonism involves a direct chemical interaction that neutralizes or inactivates another drug or toxin.","optionReasons":{"A":"Chemical antagonism involves a direct chemical interaction that neutralizes or inactivates another drug or toxin."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"94eba0286f3c004722945d28ae6d6c79b51b0559a90c5deec740be2a1273ab68"},"q-9006f49ec170":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Negative action on the fetus causing fetal malformation","explanation":"Teratogenicity refers to interference with embryonic or fetal development leading to congenital structural or functional abnormalities.","optionReasons":{"B":"Teratogenicity refers to interference with embryonic or fetal development leading to congenital structural or functional abnormalities."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"47d7a67bad38526603c4325399394fcf0fe4ac6845c38f22d258a7883e6a6e35"},"q-9a1d14116eb5":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Unpredictable, inherent, qualitatively abnormal reaction to a drug","explanation":"An idiosyncratic response is an unusual, unpredictable drug reaction related to individual susceptibility rather than the typical dose-related pharmacology.","optionReasons":{"C":"An idiosyncratic response is an unusual, unpredictable drug reaction related to individual susceptibility rather than the typical dose-related pharmacology."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5ee9a0e172658851ebcb8a4b481555fd681be1b4246e3f1a5fdbb2f865deb5b3"},"q-0fc13a96add8":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Bupivacaine","explanation":"Bupivacaine is more potent and longer-acting than lidocaine, mepivacaine or procaine among the listed local anesthetics.","optionReasons":{"B":"Bupivacaine is more potent and longer-acting than lidocaine, mepivacaine or procaine among the listed local anesthetics."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"6bcd33bdb7be0a8b265cc2b57b82542924d66992d89eb8113711e9f42e65d8bd"},"q-20033ca08bd8":{"correct":"D","answerText":"All of the above","explanation":"Higher lipid solubility of local anesthetics is commonly associated with higher potency, stronger protein binding and often prolonged action.","optionReasons":{"D":"Higher lipid solubility of local anesthetics is commonly associated with higher potency, stronger protein binding and often prolonged action."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ffb0559ca0aef66d7780ceace268ad8557d5f275171be3c53197cd5758fc3366"},"q-d72b10aa0bc3":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Motor function","explanation":"Motor fibers generally require a more intense block than small pain-transmitting fibers, making motor function relatively late to disappear.","optionReasons":{"C":"Motor fibers generally require a more intense block than small pain-transmitting fibers, making motor function relatively late to disappear."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"916e38cb462fe51f321b06b03b9446b7ee3bbaf87b01188f9d4c01371ade9667"},"q-3b7d63ad0a64":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Type A delta and C fibers","explanation":"Rapid sharp pain is conveyed predominantly by small myelinated A-delta fibers; unmyelinated C fibers mediate slower pain.","optionReasons":{"A":"Rapid sharp pain is conveyed predominantly by small myelinated A-delta fibers; unmyelinated C fibers mediate slower pain."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"733a91d39b52e8e61058df8fa7e07216882fcdc9cc341b8856f585d3947d01f1"},"q-60db190e80b4":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Procaine","explanation":"Procaine is an ester local anesthetic with relatively short action; bupivacaine, ropivacaine and tetracaine last longer.","optionReasons":{"A":"Procaine is an ester local anesthetic with relatively short action; bupivacaine, ropivacaine and tetracaine last longer."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0a9f32cf710e91bb2e23acd02847b6222e40cb0bbe88e7bb729e9a785f204951"},"q-7e83d34a2136":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Myocardial depression and peripheral vasodilatation","explanation":"Cocaine increases sympathetic activity and can cause coronary vasoconstriction and tachycardia, not typical peripheral vasodilatation with myocardial depression.","optionReasons":{"C":"Cocaine increases sympathetic activity and can cause coronary vasoconstriction and tachycardia, not typical peripheral vasodilatation with myocardial depression."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"165fcf361f5f83077c7facfa85670c4879ff52b397bca37e7b90185e4023e2af"},"q-366c6c319382":{"correct":"C","answerText":"It readily penetrates the skin and mucosa","explanation":"Procaine is an ester anesthetic with poor topical mucosal penetration, short action, and a PABA metabolite that can antagonize sulfonamides.","optionReasons":{"C":"Procaine is an ester anesthetic with poor topical mucosal penetration, short action, and a PABA metabolite that can antagonize sulfonamides."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"decf8cce846c3d06f6597ae0111eeb387f4e032b0d202c03ef6d534c805b1f25"},"q-9ad87a5564fc":{"correct":"B","answerText":"It has esteratic linkage","explanation":"Lidocaine is an amide local anesthetic, not an ester; it is also used as a class Ib antiarrhythmic.","optionReasons":{"B":"Lidocaine is an amide local anesthetic, not an ester; it is also used as a class Ib antiarrhythmic."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"e7760b0de7d5f7ad15d5f0312313ed08c7b6f802baf816ee5aed38c9342d89c5"},"q-101485e42981":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Low potency","explanation":"Tetracaine is a potent, relatively long-acting ester local anesthetic, so low potency is the incorrect description.","optionReasons":{"B":"Tetracaine is a potent, relatively long-acting ester local anesthetic, so low potency is the incorrect description."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c347fb6fa434f8566b743890d6a0004f25648ac6d8736961dfcc7035c14fa0c9"},"q-1e4991d37c31":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Sympathetic postganglionic nerve endings","explanation":"Most sympathetic postganglionic nerves release norepinephrine; sweat gland fibers are the important cholinergic exception.","optionReasons":{"B":"Most sympathetic postganglionic nerves release norepinephrine; sweat gland fibers are the important cholinergic exception."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"85b9b7180088ed6aaf3744d1cb03ee3e97ee285b284b31ffc9c07bbdf64fab4c"},"q-ef3b9e7b1b13":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Autonomic effector cells","explanation":"Muscarinic receptors mediate parasympathetic signaling on many effector organs such as smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands.","optionReasons":{"C":"Muscarinic receptors mediate parasympathetic signaling on many effector organs such as smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"fbf72842ba4a426c5500446dcac9f5790526f20e32004f66b93a4008e8121cbc"},"q-caa90f24cbc6":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Heart","explanation":"Cardiac M2 muscarinic receptors slow sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodal activity when stimulated.","optionReasons":{"A":"Cardiac M2 muscarinic receptors slow sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodal activity when stimulated."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"cb85103f63fe7db399d3c210497404fee4b6ff177d917f1f0b37ab48ba1a1b28"},"q-28f542345a0a":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Dryness of mouth, hyperpyrexia, hallucination","explanation":"Muscarinic mushroom poisoning typically causes excess secretions, bradycardia and gastrointestinal symptoms; dry mouth, heat and hallucinations suggest antimuscarinic toxicity instead.","optionReasons":{"B":"Muscarinic mushroom poisoning typically causes excess secretions, bradycardia and gastrointestinal symptoms; dry mouth, heat and hallucinations suggest antimuscarinic toxicity instead."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"cc104ea891c119386751288cc317a8486b4e0a465a40be8a3903a81e3e573e8d"},"q-5d0b97a9a96f":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Carbachol","explanation":"Carbachol directly activates muscarinic and nicotinic receptors; edrophonium and physostigmine inhibit acetylcholinesterase.","optionReasons":{"C":"Carbachol directly activates muscarinic and nicotinic receptors; edrophonium and physostigmine inhibit acetylcholinesterase."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"cb2140984d7b7d10c9a78ea5b2f3b56ad33ed0d70ddcea4b2647c7b7e4b078c0"},"q-4138d40ed8a6":{"correct":"B","answerText":"It causes mydriasis","explanation":"Carbachol induces miosis, not mydriasis; it also resists cholinesterase degradation and can lower intraocular pressure.","optionReasons":{"B":"Carbachol induces miosis, not mydriasis; it also resists cholinesterase degradation and can lower intraocular pressure."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"2d44ad5e48d09f3320402d61a718b5d7544069abec1c5df1e8a7e9eb6cacf15c"},"q-d67ee19c3624":{"correct":"C","answerText":"It is very rapidly hydrolyzed","explanation":"Acetylcholine has a very short duration because acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes it rapidly.","optionReasons":{"C":"Acetylcholine has a very short duration because acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes it rapidly."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"4ab9df2a838c7e04657ac019a98177ba48ad0a04fe9dabc604ae37617cea8a94"},"q-293893f61f34":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Bradycardia","explanation":"Parasympathomimetics activate cardiac M2 receptors and decrease heart rate; they generally cause miosis and increased gut motility.","optionReasons":{"C":"Parasympathomimetics activate cardiac M2 receptors and decrease heart rate; they generally cause miosis and increased gut motility."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"064be10d503717c2e1a08fc1e0cd801c5b174dc9039c79e95a7fdbeb0ef32048"},"q-110945c7ac69":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Acetylcholine","explanation":"Acetylcholine is rapidly hydrolyzed by cholinesterases and thus acts for a very short time.","optionReasons":{"A":"Acetylcholine is rapidly hydrolyzed by cholinesterases and thus acts for a very short time."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"8009f986d722db6788b838f53a1d9838ce452338944f16d901638dde628e8cc7"},"q-f9b6c33bc226":{"correct":"D","answerText":"It exerts both nicotinic and muscarinic effects","explanation":"Bethanechol is a muscarinic-selective agonist without appreciable nicotinic activity, so the claimed dual action is incorrect.","optionReasons":{"D":"Bethanechol is a muscarinic-selective agonist without appreciable nicotinic activity, so the claimed dual action is incorrect."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"6a1db295eef0adb59c17b26fba9269d9cbb943ad22898887a9df71f39c979cee"},"q-0db50f052361":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Causes a decrease in secretory and motor activity of gut","explanation":"Pilocarpine stimulates muscarinic secretion and gastrointestinal motility rather than decreasing them.","optionReasons":{"C":"Pilocarpine stimulates muscarinic secretion and gastrointestinal motility rather than decreasing them."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"8013baa9bf3f9e134421dd47c299f007121e85d9252982255a402effff16c7d0"},"q-b0944df6cde9":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Lobeline","explanation":"Lobeline is a plant-derived nicotinic agonist with weaker nicotinic activity than nicotine.","optionReasons":{"A":"Lobeline is a plant-derived nicotinic agonist with weaker nicotinic activity than nicotine."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"9414794ae3a49f1db1fe85aed17e3652d566a7b917ea9bed2c6e5802d78261bb"},"q-ee552048616e":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Edrophonium","explanation":"Edrophonium inhibits acetylcholinesterase to increase endogenous acetylcholine rather than directly stimulating receptors.","optionReasons":{"B":"Edrophonium inhibits acetylcholinesterase to increase endogenous acetylcholine rather than directly stimulating receptors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ff4a7bb2d6dd1dd08bf948786fe2c67786dcff71de38a38fe5e058855922848c"},"q-0fb11f285e5c":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Inhibition of the hydrolysis of endogenous acetylcholine","explanation":"Indirect cholinomimetics prevent breakdown of endogenous acetylcholine through acetylcholinesterase inhibition.","optionReasons":{"B":"Indirect cholinomimetics prevent breakdown of endogenous acetylcholine through acetylcholinesterase inhibition."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"2f212fcebf5f8755eb2f740d926d62304da6987e7992ce99cac114ab760f0311"},"q-8fd09d3c5edc":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Physostigmine","explanation":"Physostigmine is a reversible carbamate acetylcholinesterase inhibitor; organophosphates are typically irreversible.","optionReasons":{"C":"Physostigmine is a reversible carbamate acetylcholinesterase inhibitor; organophosphates are typically irreversible."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3475deeb339053f0c0763810e1eeaa577a58793985eabb355680165b8690675d"},"q-0249fc52ef8b":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Isoflurophate","explanation":"Isofluorophate (DFP) is an organophosphate that inactivates acetylcholinesterase for a prolonged period.","optionReasons":{"D":"Isofluorophate (DFP) is an organophosphate that inactivates acetylcholinesterase for a prolonged period."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ebf8e86a0ed617dc3c043e0738cb7e3507ea55ce2e1f64a0f6d816a1f112fa6c"},"q-2985bebe57ca":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Pralidoxime","explanation":"Pralidoxime reactivates phosphorylated acetylcholinesterase before enzyme aging in certain organophosphate poisonings.","optionReasons":{"A":"Pralidoxime reactivates phosphorylated acetylcholinesterase before enzyme aging in certain organophosphate poisonings."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"699ff3d3c1752ac896f8bdab34066464c784259dda86bcb2bd796d2642b4186b"},"q-51254bd1272b":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Bronchodilation","explanation":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibition raises acetylcholine, which promotes bronchoconstriction rather than bronchodilation.","optionReasons":{"B":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibition raises acetylcholine, which promotes bronchoconstriction rather than bronchodilation."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"874525d6cf34c790c40562f9b7267f6aef643c6f98bf9b64c1f6fbd0b4de9da2"},"q-8e33b4247028":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Neostigmine","explanation":"Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase and also has direct nicotinic agonist activity at the neuromuscular junction.","optionReasons":{"C":"Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase and also has direct nicotinic agonist activity at the neuromuscular junction."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"43690e3beb8657bd785b55372397ee174c18d0f65985bfb4469fb318c4af0192"},"q-21865c10da1b":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Dramatic hypertension and tachycardia","explanation":"Excess cholinergic signaling ordinarily produces bradycardia and miosis; dramatic tachycardia and hypertension are not the expected pattern.","optionReasons":{"D":"Excess cholinergic signaling ordinarily produces bradycardia and miosis; dramatic tachycardia and hypertension are not the expected pattern."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a74d8f71af9aa91e311ef94a8f01734d6b66289d4d6fecee9af440bade2dab40"},"q-020615299581":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Neostigmine","explanation":"Neostigmine can increase gastrointestinal and bladder smooth muscle activity in selected nonobstructive atony states.","optionReasons":{"B":"Neostigmine can increase gastrointestinal and bladder smooth muscle activity in selected nonobstructive atony states."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"b23a2892d519496810bd8ac6a20532f97f951977ee27a7d213670a7283b7ed65"},"q-a4934be1aafe":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Physostigmine","explanation":"Physostigmine is a tertiary amine that crosses the blood–brain barrier; edrophonium and neostigmine penetrate poorly.","optionReasons":{"A":"Physostigmine is a tertiary amine that crosses the blood–brain barrier; edrophonium and neostigmine penetrate poorly."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"37f5a3593b77b399a0baf62eae6da0e90ee6562993042c1773bccb02fa64077a"},"q-bdc7ee0df495":{"correct":"C","answerText":"On effector cell membranes of glandular and smooth muscle cells","explanation":"M3 receptors are widely expressed in exocrine glands and visceral smooth muscle and mediate secretion and contraction.","optionReasons":{"C":"M3 receptors are widely expressed in exocrine glands and visceral smooth muscle and mediate secretion and contraction."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"e1277fdcf7254a1c57f3aec5b86af0226b4b05d5f1c47ad6ea513fcb917eccf0"},"q-ab284cfed6ba":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Scopolamine","explanation":"Scopolamine is an antimuscarinic drug; pilocarpine is a muscarinic agonist and pipecuronium blocks skeletal neuromuscular receptors.","optionReasons":{"A":"Scopolamine is an antimuscarinic drug; pilocarpine is a muscarinic agonist and pipecuronium blocks skeletal neuromuscular receptors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"18e93e7570ddf3347732fbd6f09e8173ef78e443b0011d45dfc3a2d04c3ff055"},"q-1336f3563852":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Pancuronium","explanation":"Pancuronium is a nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker that competitively antagonizes nicotinic Nm receptors.","optionReasons":{"A":"Pancuronium is a nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker that competitively antagonizes nicotinic Nm receptors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5e0a3d9e5955d51f88da872498bf352d9b37a9921b62d15885a0f0513639fbd7"},"q-55fc33b142d9":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Scopolamine","explanation":"Scopolamine is lipophilic and enters the CNS, causing more pronounced central effects than many peripheral antimuscarinics.","optionReasons":{"B":"Scopolamine is lipophilic and enters the CNS, causing more pronounced central effects than many peripheral antimuscarinics."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5c368dbf056b3db98dcfe9262786acc6c9cbd65797f4c43b4c74528448878b0b"},"q-794523e2ea8d":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Eye","explanation":"Atropine-induced mydriasis and cycloplegia may persist substantially longer than effects on heart rate or secretions.","optionReasons":{"A":"Atropine-induced mydriasis and cycloplegia may persist substantially longer than effects on heart rate or secretions."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3ec3dd29dcd766fbf3f780234516630f7eacd5d6b8d18816a78c06218ddbeb24"},"q-4535b36038a5":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Competitive muscarinic blockade","explanation":"Atropine is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors and does not block nicotinic ganglionic receptors.","optionReasons":{"B":"Atropine is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors and does not block nicotinic ganglionic receptors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"9398d88d0807328ccee16d622ee4670d6eab11b45c561eacafedf94af9886e09"},"q-a088b1dce4dc":{"correct":"A","answerText":"The salivary, bronchial and sweat glands","explanation":"Salivary, bronchial and sweat secretions are particularly sensitive to antimuscarinic inhibition.","optionReasons":{"A":"Salivary, bronchial and sweat secretions are particularly sensitive to antimuscarinic inhibition."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"736e1c58c908bd4bc4bec62a54a6ac56800f7c73ab3e236d0566d50af45813d3"},"q-1302f3e5037a":{"correct":"D","answerText":"All of the above","explanation":"Atropine is broadly nonselective at muscarinic receptor subtypes, including M1, M2 and M3.","optionReasons":{"D":"Atropine is broadly nonselective at muscarinic receptor subtypes, including M1, M2 and M3."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"b37e235dcd5b417c7c47e96196230a8ab1ccd4b8fea4a1a6d4c0dc4c718bc735"},"q-b5b0bf53b23c":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Scopolamine","explanation":"Scopolamine is a centrally active antimuscarinic effective against vestibular motion sickness.","optionReasons":{"C":"Scopolamine is a centrally active antimuscarinic effective against vestibular motion sickness."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"599040b48952c4ed648d8d495c7dd8a327d8775e9997dd0c6321a81897052771"},"q-adcd3ee61328":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Atropine","explanation":"Atropine reduces lacrimal secretion and can cause dry, gritty or sandy eyes.","optionReasons":{"A":"Atropine reduces lacrimal secretion and can cause dry, gritty or sandy eyes."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ff63e600c9a95c76797d7aa1fba1fd6b51197eb9c7fef7544db56992d04f032a"},"q-3f5909f1b242":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Ventricle","explanation":"Atrial tissue and nodal conduction are sensitive to vagal muscarinic tone; ventricles are relatively less directly sensitive.","optionReasons":{"D":"Atrial tissue and nodal conduction are sensitive to vagal muscarinic tone; ventricles are relatively less directly sensitive."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"8cab9c6e14c7996514164276feeab93c4bad33e75097f74386a2b4037d8281e7"},"q-8c2b56725963":{"correct":"D","answerText":"All of the above","explanation":"Atropine has limited peptic-ulcer usefulness because effective doses cause many antimuscarinic adverse effects and impair gastric motility.","optionReasons":{"D":"Atropine has limited peptic-ulcer usefulness because effective doses cause many antimuscarinic adverse effects and impair gastric motility."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"10964a3ab4296533dca8ba9b3f96fda00ee03eab794b37d5d86a12d70b8553cf"},"q-5c28f6b2681d":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Pirenzepine","explanation":"Pirenzepine preferentially blocks M1 muscarinic receptors compared with the nonselective agents listed.","optionReasons":{"C":"Pirenzepine preferentially blocks M1 muscarinic receptors compared with the nonselective agents listed."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ec8b7788639b9a73694c614e1d13258c8de05f90b270c5c450a7dc5b5fde7bd4"},"q-f08f6607cac6":{"correct":"C","answerText":"In infants and children","explanation":"Young children have a higher risk of antimuscarinic hyperthermia because sweating is inhibited, impairing heat loss.","optionReasons":{"C":"Young children have a higher risk of antimuscarinic hyperthermia because sweating is inhibited, impairing heat loss."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1bcc765953b59454f1dc32c178afa76a49b6281fb14036e62290e8c5f7f0697b"},"q-a27fea7b62fa":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Benztropine","explanation":"Benztropine is a centrally acting antimuscarinic that can reduce tremor in Parkinson disease; it does not replace dopaminergic therapy.","optionReasons":{"A":"Benztropine is a centrally acting antimuscarinic that can reduce tremor in Parkinson disease; it does not replace dopaminergic therapy."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"60f2855a17bee01eb10338034fdef563cce2bdab1b5a310fd4ac67d348240948"},"q-a09c2842d405":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Homatropine","explanation":"Homatropine is an ophthalmic antimuscarinic that dilates the pupil (mydriasis).","optionReasons":{"C":"Homatropine is an ophthalmic antimuscarinic that dilates the pupil (mydriasis)."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"378627dd6f73b84a960bc407eca84f802506ae5361c9a851639d14285f9f8ba7"},"q-f6b06b84358b":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Ipratropium","explanation":"Ipratropium is an inhaled muscarinic antagonist producing bronchodilation by blocking airway M3 receptors.","optionReasons":{"B":"Ipratropium is an inhaled muscarinic antagonist producing bronchodilation by blocking airway M3 receptors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"36d2fab917a56043edda455b03f233472b75c5beb886de7f29e8863d2cfeb442"},"q-c5eca3752838":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Glaucoma","explanation":"Antimuscarinic eye drops can increase intraocular pressure in susceptible patients with angle-closure glaucoma; they are not a routine glaucoma treatment.","optionReasons":{"B":"Antimuscarinic eye drops can increase intraocular pressure in susceptible patients with angle-closure glaucoma; they are not a routine glaucoma treatment."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"2fbe2e8ebc001cc83b7bc3b6228c58f9533e93fdd540a43231ef22eba7e3880f"},"q-c7db10597e35":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Bradicardia, orthostatic hypotension","explanation":"Antimuscarinic toxicity typically causes tachycardia, not bradycardia; other features include dry skin, hyperthermia and delirium.","optionReasons":{"D":"Antimuscarinic toxicity typically causes tachycardia, not bradycardia; other features include dry skin, hyperthermia and delirium."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"deb8ef199083718ab0defa0b65fec0bed97a44abd4404e7215d0612f6f20684f"},"q-d778118324e5":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Bronchial asthma","explanation":"Bronchial asthma is not a contraindication to antimuscarinic therapy; inhaled ipratropium may be beneficial.","optionReasons":{"C":"Bronchial asthma is not a contraindication to antimuscarinic therapy; inhaled ipratropium may be beneficial."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ad8275b3263a471ea16981c69d318fac204480e7c96270023fa05923af05bdc8"},"q-43c415aa4c83":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Autonomic ganglia","explanation":"Hexamethonium blocks nicotinic neuronal receptors in autonomic ganglia, interrupting sympathetic and parasympathetic transmission.","optionReasons":{"C":"Hexamethonium blocks nicotinic neuronal receptors in autonomic ganglia, interrupting sympathetic and parasympathetic transmission."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"e20b04b06f7826b390f453ea00414cc279d4632f369b7a4d551683713b4cd9dc"},"q-33be1be7bb06":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Trimethaphane","explanation":"Trimethaphan is a short-acting ganglionic blocker formerly used intravenously for rapid blood-pressure control.","optionReasons":{"B":"Trimethaphan is a short-acting ganglionic blocker formerly used intravenously for rapid blood-pressure control."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"d8cfb0c87e9de3125c69887052d8eee7b669108574b3970192ed6a4ba68f1e74"},"q-bb7f138dd900":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Mecamylamine","explanation":"Mecamylamine is an orally active ganglionic blocker; trimethaphan is given intravenously.","optionReasons":{"A":"Mecamylamine is an orally active ganglionic blocker; trimethaphan is given intravenously."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0530a26d7b9838ec10df91cd35c889bfa298e9c3e955a1b9758c07c8256d7cb3"},"q-636e15f5569b":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Stimulation of thermoregulatory sweating","explanation":"Ganglionic blockade generally diminishes sympathetic sweating instead of stimulating thermoregulatory perspiration.","optionReasons":{"D":"Ganglionic blockade generally diminishes sympathetic sweating instead of stimulating thermoregulatory perspiration."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3dcf5cebafcc57751ebd93dce5d70b793bf99a28d63fc5e1206e15149e95520a"},"q-6413d3dbd510":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Cardiovascular collapse","explanation":"Ganglionic blockers lower blood pressure and may be used to induce hypotension; they would worsen cardiovascular collapse.","optionReasons":{"C":"Ganglionic blockers lower blood pressure and may be used to induce hypotension; they would worsen cardiovascular collapse."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"6843fe783930ff26792a9ab971eee48f5525fdad5e5cdb38e47f7de268c29f53"},"q-21961f10f2cd":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Interaction of acetylcholine with cholinergic receptors","explanation":"Neuromuscular blockers prevent transmission at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on skeletal muscle end plates.","optionReasons":{"A":"Neuromuscular blockers prevent transmission at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on skeletal muscle end plates."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"72c8d595d236b4823427027cef291f74878c4d54109b069cf6b6f4e5d731e891"},"q-92386d6c60ef":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Prevent access of the transmitter to its receptor and depolarization","explanation":"Nondepolarizing neuromuscular blockers compete with acetylcholine for Nm receptors without opening ion channels.","optionReasons":{"B":"Nondepolarizing neuromuscular blockers compete with acetylcholine for Nm receptors without opening ion channels."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1d7a8ac729e2bf0f9fa2e26a953d97842ddf82063ff3e45e63f39dd9d6030567"},"q-8df4d36cf114":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Succylcholine","explanation":"Succinylcholine consists structurally of two linked acetylcholine-like moieties.","optionReasons":{"D":"Succinylcholine consists structurally of two linked acetylcholine-like moieties."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c84ad77ec90146a50d97d07bbe95124dbe33380df8ea84f1b47f9be6ad9bbab6"},"q-b5fff1e7c763":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Vecuronium","explanation":"Vecuronium has an intermediate duration of neuromuscular blockade; pancuronium and tubocurarine are longer acting.","optionReasons":{"A":"Vecuronium has an intermediate duration of neuromuscular blockade; pancuronium and tubocurarine are longer acting."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"dff9230136129dc65bd27c8317b107ad775c7be7553dd0ee4d30c6e2992da62c"},"q-d8083e393124":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Mivacurium","explanation":"Mivacurium is a short-acting nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker rapidly hydrolyzed by plasma cholinesterase.","optionReasons":{"C":"Mivacurium is a short-acting nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocker rapidly hydrolyzed by plasma cholinesterase."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5933ca8d74b3cfd9731dc51b396c47881f4d2800c1c43a9260e7cf965e8b994f"},"q-f07cd5585c48":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Succinylcholine","explanation":"Succinylcholine is a depolarizing relaxant of very short duration in patients with normal pseudocholinesterase function.","optionReasons":{"D":"Succinylcholine is a depolarizing relaxant of very short duration in patients with normal pseudocholinesterase function."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"56b495f9f1e9d00651126f8b107fbec030bf79f608af09d3c92a157d64000f32"},"q-8c5f6868e3e7":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Both b and c","explanation":"Both succinylcholine and mivacurium depend on plasma cholinesterase, so enzyme variants or substantial liver dysfunction can prolong blockade.","optionReasons":{"D":"Both succinylcholine and mivacurium depend on plasma cholinesterase, so enzyme variants or substantial liver dysfunction can prolong blockade."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0309fb21adb7223676a693de06e7ae2a3fccb36e64c8e30ce17d5f36d5c3fa63"},"q-8219cc533467":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Interact with nicotinic receptor to compete with acetylcholine without receptor activation","explanation":"Depolarizing agents initially activate Nm receptors and depolarize the endplate; simple competitive blockade without activation describes nondepolarizers.","optionReasons":{"A":"Depolarizing agents initially activate Nm receptors and depolarize the endplate; simple competitive blockade without activation describes nondepolarizers."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"8d533e9e73b7685980b7a26429775c40a34310ae794419191fd2c8ebd1fc0b43"},"q-a376a27418d3":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Tubocurarine","explanation":"Tubocurarine can cause histamine release, leading to flushing, hypotension and occasionally bronchospasm.","optionReasons":{"B":"Tubocurarine can cause histamine release, leading to flushing, hypotension and occasionally bronchospasm."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"b37e35dd1641dd9de2b8002c2af7c7b645d2b48f5a5f694fd4fce8e0cae16b74"},"q-6f2ba1fb7d01":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Tubocurarine","explanation":"Tubocurarine can trigger histamine release and hypotension or bronchospasm; vecuronium typically has fewer such effects.","optionReasons":{"C":"Tubocurarine can trigger histamine release and hypotension or bronchospasm; vecuronium typically has fewer such effects."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"faf61e07ee18edea6bd9927ec3945b0411d057150579977520bdc4eb587ed825"},"q-eac83b28af7b":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Pancuronium","explanation":"Pancuronium has vagolytic properties that can increase heart rate and produce tachycardia.","optionReasons":{"C":"Pancuronium has vagolytic properties that can increase heart rate and produce tachycardia."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3b70280cac829ebc04d386457c55a586fd60ef44f379d3ee3d29c3893ee7d032"},"q-024a6eda37aa":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Succinylcholine","explanation":"Succinylcholine can cause vagally mediated bradyarrhythmias and potassium-related ventricular dysrhythmias.","optionReasons":{"D":"Succinylcholine can cause vagally mediated bradyarrhythmias and potassium-related ventricular dysrhythmias."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"85b3e7f132e9e138bf083aa48b4dcc5056960c942ffd016cb2162abc62ba9c1e"},"q-e55cdffac165":{"correct":"B","answerText":"A decrease in intraocular pressure","explanation":"Succinylcholine may transiently increase intraocular pressure; therefore a fall in pressure is not a characteristic depolarizing-blocker effect.","optionReasons":{"B":"Succinylcholine may transiently increase intraocular pressure; therefore a fall in pressure is not a characteristic depolarizing-blocker effect."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"de8ff85c80fd74ce7bb7acedd8adb415625a09274c01bfa12e630b7906093850"},"q-bd0ac75f66da":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Succinylcholine","explanation":"Succinylcholine can raise intraocular pressure; the exam's categorical wording should be treated cautiously in modern practice.","optionReasons":{"B":"Succinylcholine can raise intraocular pressure; the exam's categorical wording should be treated cautiously in modern practice."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"973fd4bd43314d914149a1079ab0c794d5afab4ac357a2f8d52579c6c80824a0"},"q-3690e6b60771":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Pipecuronium","explanation":"Pipecuronium is substantially renally eliminated and may accumulate during renal failure; atracurium undergoes organ-independent breakdown.","optionReasons":{"A":"Pipecuronium is substantially renally eliminated and may accumulate during renal failure; atracurium undergoes organ-independent breakdown."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"e3bfe270d686885827f66e59c87ae15f76398797553207771ad5fb62e857a8fe"},"q-5db8f41b0b3d":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Neostigmine","explanation":"Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine available to outcompete nondepolarizing agents at Nm receptors.","optionReasons":{"B":"Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine available to outcompete nondepolarizing agents at Nm receptors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1765b4a695be0b38b0de05f491b2be75f702dfc104e723181147a25ae37d0265"},"q-3c1095e618cb":{"correct":"D","answerText":"All of the above","explanation":"Sympathetic effects result from norepinephrine release, adrenoceptor activation and circulating adrenal epinephrine, making all listed components relevant.","optionReasons":{"D":"Sympathetic effects result from norepinephrine release, adrenoceptor activation and circulating adrenal epinephrine, making all listed components relevant."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"443c7b721d183be3526f3a7a0f832a093907c95a0a4adf17f3f00faf4318f094"},"q-cb3da1358839":{"correct":"B","answerText":"It is synthesized into the nerve ending","explanation":"Epinephrine is synthesized chiefly in adrenal chromaffin cells and circulates as a hormone; most sympathetic nerve endings release norepinephrine.","optionReasons":{"B":"Epinephrine is synthesized chiefly in adrenal chromaffin cells and circulates as a hormone; most sympathetic nerve endings release norepinephrine."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"4ef36818be2d6bcde6ae5aa2f3ef41e372045930338e6cf9090444feb94e242a"},"q-449b886999f8":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Ephedrine","explanation":"Ephedrine acts partly indirectly by promoting norepinephrine release, unlike the directly acting agonists listed.","optionReasons":{"C":"Ephedrine acts partly indirectly by promoting norepinephrine release, unlike the directly acting agonists listed."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"81f3d33e1cd95dfb1b35e9a2ea7b6474b6d63414d9495b643401b9dfb2c74314"},"q-066a8995e695":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Translation","explanation":"Translation occurs at ribosomes, where mRNA codons specify the amino acid sequence of a protein; transcription produces RNA from DNA.","optionReasons":{"A":"Translation occurs at ribosomes, where mRNA codons specify the amino acid sequence of a protein; transcription produces RNA from DNA."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"cc7757d50f81e0163e95edd2594f5a6e44f85d8c2300eab958e3ea9166aeac19"},"q-2dc92e2e86c3":{"correct":"C","answerText":"50:1","explanation":"Lowering pH by one unit increases the weak-acid-to-conjugate-base ratio tenfold; 5:1 becomes 50:1 by the Henderson–Hasselbalch relationship.","optionReasons":{"C":"Lowering pH by one unit increases the weak-acid-to-conjugate-base ratio tenfold; 5:1 becomes 50:1 by the Henderson–Hasselbalch relationship."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"6c8bfacac3f7ad520dda46df751eb8e37a688c082e24e32b72ec6be25fe2d9a6"},"q-5746f4acc6e6":{"correct":"D","answerText":"the components of a buffer are conjugate pairs.","explanation":"A buffer consists of a weak acid and its conjugate base or a weak base and its conjugate acid, allowing it to resist pH changes.","optionReasons":{"D":"A buffer consists of a weak acid and its conjugate base or a weak base and its conjugate acid, allowing it to resist pH changes."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"df4e2473c5f5c60979d6e3bb0f96cbfed5d9ac81475b1c6a26a37710a9289e56"},"q-e95022d1c79c":{"correct":"A","answerText":"function as co-enzymes.","explanation":"Lipids provide energy storage, structural phospholipids and precursors of steroid hormones and eicosanoids; general coenzyme catalysis is not their main function.","optionReasons":{"A":"Lipids provide energy storage, structural phospholipids and precursors of steroid hormones and eicosanoids; general coenzyme catalysis is not their main function."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"2d4a569fc9db2387db9cf1f212c285065aacdb9fa83be050c39e34d96cdff439"},"q-bbbcd6daf028":{"correct":"D","answerText":"monosaccharides","explanation":"Monosaccharides can cross cell membranes through specific transporters; large di- and polysaccharides generally cannot pass intact.","optionReasons":{"D":"Monosaccharides can cross cell membranes through specific transporters; large di- and polysaccharides generally cannot pass intact."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"aab0d42a7a4934bbe0ae68481bd1fb0a21541499dbc8247cf2e19987696276ec"},"q-9076e61267fe":{"correct":"A","answerText":"monosaccharide","explanation":"Fructose is a single sugar unit, so it is a monosaccharide rather than an oligo- or polysaccharide.","optionReasons":{"A":"Fructose is a single sugar unit, so it is a monosaccharide rather than an oligo- or polysaccharide."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"f3416cc458335f2332ccec6f4a6e9512eaef9fc54e166681ed182b7578062483"},"q-5534defb37f2":{"correct":"A","answerText":"monounsaturated","explanation":"Oleic acid has one carbon–carbon double bond and is therefore monounsaturated, not polyunsaturated.","optionReasons":{"A":"Oleic acid has one carbon–carbon double bond and is therefore monounsaturated, not polyunsaturated."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1e4fbf3dea8560fdbd0dd8133422b51992f7f58e0c322c0479d65cdd0cbbdba0"},"q-7c813bfe0bae":{"correct":"C","answerText":"disaccharide","explanation":"Disaccharides consist of two sugar units and are carbohydrates; a dipeptide is a small protein fragment.","optionReasons":{"C":"Disaccharides consist of two sugar units and are carbohydrates; a dipeptide is a small protein fragment."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"524d36d4c761feee3a916198395db9b30f459daefa54a8a54d1521f5effb992a"},"q-a62e597255f0":{"correct":"B","answerText":"to provide energy","explanation":"Carbohydrates, especially glucose, provide metabolic fuel for ATP synthesis.","optionReasons":{"B":"Carbohydrates, especially glucose, provide metabolic fuel for ATP synthesis."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"749062ac90dbfa7059e0a830c07489f0bc11b88796efd2803ef62dadb726a09c"},"q-5d7424cd7cdc":{"correct":"A","answerText":"triglycerols","explanation":"Most dietary fat occurs as triacylglycerols (triglycerides), formed from glycerol esterified with three fatty acids.","optionReasons":{"A":"Most dietary fat occurs as triacylglycerols (triglycerides), formed from glycerol esterified with three fatty acids."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c769271b81489aa5340e8a4dfaa80f7ce2e08c75ad331cd4a961c7c583943eaf"},"q-4ce22096745f":{"correct":"A","answerText":"primary structure","explanation":"The primary structure of a protein is its linear amino acid sequence, linked by peptide bonds.","optionReasons":{"A":"The primary structure of a protein is its linear amino acid sequence, linked by peptide bonds."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"93589ee749322660096cc471a1718f86df54c3b1f02ec52b27d38818561768d3"},"q-99cc65525104":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Require the dose increase of some drugs","explanation":"Enzyme induction accelerates elimination of certain drugs, sometimes requiring a higher maintenance dose to maintain exposure.","optionReasons":{"A":"Enzyme induction accelerates elimination of certain drugs, sometimes requiring a higher maintenance dose to maintain exposure."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5652ff222d8b3315476a23faab6aef7e055d217fe21261e99723def836a03cd0"},"q-4e8baef620ed":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Transformation of substances due to oxidation, reduction or hydrolysis","explanation":"Phase I metabolism primarily involves oxidation, reduction or hydrolysis, while acetylation and glucuronidation are conjugation reactions.","optionReasons":{"B":"Phase I metabolism primarily involves oxidation, reduction or hydrolysis, while acetylation and glucuronidation are conjugation reactions."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c3352ede4a5c8b2736802533f6ef3ed5763c51464e32ced75c6911f14943b19f"},"q-d8beb5e1d5c9":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Metabolism of local anesthetics occurs at the site of administration","explanation":"Esters are generally hydrolyzed by plasma esterases, and amides by hepatic enzymes; substantial local tissue metabolism is not the standard route for most commonly used agents.","optionReasons":{"A":"Esters are generally hydrolyzed by plasma esterases, and amides by hepatic enzymes; substantial local tissue metabolism is not the standard route for most commonly used agents."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"d794acdaea12715c2e7c0cd58c21c6f01a867ff773e2e32c2b6128aabca4f980"},"q-6da5289409f6":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Barbiturate tolerance","explanation":"Barbiturates induce hepatic drug-metabolizing enzymes, which can contribute to metabolic tolerance during repeated exposure.","optionReasons":{"A":"Barbiturates induce hepatic drug-metabolizing enzymes, which can contribute to metabolic tolerance during repeated exposure."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a0c8e3c81162407ee259142073ac4e29d1873c40b325fb363d039b1e172ff811"},"q-89175f2a99eb":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Cimetidin","explanation":"Cimetidine inhibits several hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes and can slow metabolism of certain sedative drugs.","optionReasons":{"B":"Cimetidine inhibits several hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes and can slow metabolism of certain sedative drugs."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"6219bc0f1d29e912d27281894c42b2a2b8f46ef2a47de839b63c7adb66a4581a"},"q-507cfd679c24":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Pyridoxine","explanation":"Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) increases peripheral decarboxylation of levodopa if no peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor is given, reducing levodopa reaching the brain.","optionReasons":{"A":"Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) increases peripheral decarboxylation of levodopa if no peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor is given, reducing levodopa reaching the brain."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"2fee8b4938396a88427dcdccff3150cef43fed8b5d6739116ac2f51949f48bfd"},"q-6116ea179815":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Tolcapone","explanation":"Tolcapone inhibits catechol-O-methyltransferase and decreases peripheral levodopa breakdown; it also acts centrally and carries hepatotoxicity risk.","optionReasons":{"C":"Tolcapone inhibits catechol-O-methyltransferase and decreases peripheral levodopa breakdown; it also acts centrally and carries hepatotoxicity risk."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"cc39551b701420e9032cbdc8cf49019791a6aa7b12e46bd19a64b71d8079085d"},"q-c9caee30add1":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Severe loss of potassium and magnesium","explanation":"Chronic alcohol misuse may lead to magnesium and potassium deficiency through poor intake, gastrointestinal losses and renal wasting.","optionReasons":{"C":"Chronic alcohol misuse may lead to magnesium and potassium deficiency through poor intake, gastrointestinal losses and renal wasting."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"160ff5493cb4c98576f0583b04d02d9d4e55379b9f47e7127565e11b33133b16"},"q-c3a2967f2ddf":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Tranylcypramine","explanation":"Tranylcypromine irreversibly inhibits both monoamine oxidase A and B, resulting in effects persisting beyond the plasma drug half-life.","optionReasons":{"B":"Tranylcypromine irreversibly inhibits both monoamine oxidase A and B, resulting in effects persisting beyond the plasma drug half-life."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"b099f99162415e539291f68f09783a631938adeae2c59d6a75c8784fa35dc697"},"q-7425ed60fb97":{"correct":"B","answerText":"By xanthelasma under the eyes suggesting hypercholesterolemia","explanation":"Xanthelasma consists of cholesterol-rich deposits around eyelids and may be associated with dyslipidemia, although it can occur with normal lipids.","optionReasons":{"B":"Xanthelasma consists of cholesterol-rich deposits around eyelids and may be associated with dyslipidemia, although it can occur with normal lipids."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"85d78d0ca10e08a57246d6686c502271fd76b2dc3637b097ea8328a858c73120"},"q-2a276cccb473":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Folic acid","explanation":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase needs thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoate, CoA, FAD and NAD+; folate is not one of the five core cofactors.","optionReasons":{"B":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase needs thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoate, CoA, FAD and NAD+; folate is not one of the five core cofactors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1953275dfb81cf4020fa510f14e4bc9209a91af64a236bda4c78524966df8cda"},"q-641a60045ee6":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Alanine, glycine and serine","explanation":"Pancreatic elastase preferentially cleaves near small neutral side chains such as alanine, glycine and serine.","optionReasons":{"A":"Pancreatic elastase preferentially cleaves near small neutral side chains such as alanine, glycine and serine."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"99d687290ea2f9460d7e5404d0a71b1662a21fe476c26509cae8ffcadd4c0ae7"},"q-c994fdc7e82d":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Aminotransferase enzymes require pyridoxal phosphate as a co-enzyme.","explanation":"Aminotransferases require pyridoxal phosphate, the active form of vitamin B6, to transfer amino groups between metabolites.","optionReasons":{"C":"Aminotransferases require pyridoxal phosphate, the active form of vitamin B6, to transfer amino groups between metabolites."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"f8f862a5dc1def4e917191cacc49bba641f3c7c3d3f2040a290fa004bbf99d43"},"q-9e495c8d1da5":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Serine","explanation":"Creatine is synthesized using glycine, arginine and methyl groups from S-adenosylmethionine derived from methionine; serine is not a direct precursor.","optionReasons":{"B":"Creatine is synthesized using glycine, arginine and methyl groups from S-adenosylmethionine derived from methionine; serine is not a direct precursor."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5c396b1bbe046e6cc11f36ce556f97eefb28b691254d956030b4023bed625057"},"q-298a24ccaabb":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Histidine","explanation":"Histidine undergoes decarboxylation to form histamine, a mediator of allergy and inflammation.","optionReasons":{"A":"Histidine undergoes decarboxylation to form histamine, a mediator of allergy and inflammation."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"162286a417906f3e563a4149e46477953788e94567cb16922f09e12af318107d"},"q-2886080eadbf":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Leucine","explanation":"Leucine is exclusively ketogenic; isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine have both glucogenic and ketogenic pathways.","optionReasons":{"B":"Leucine is exclusively ketogenic; isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine have both glucogenic and ketogenic pathways."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"418d0a9e1279e4d27d29af205187af14c0fd910be60175410d5b60d7a0cb5470"},"q-5ecb00129fd3":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Tryptophan","explanation":"Tryptophan can be metabolized through the kynurenine pathway to form niacin equivalents.","optionReasons":{"C":"Tryptophan can be metabolized through the kynurenine pathway to form niacin equivalents."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c9c264c0d2747d6d82840d936391aad7a452dad25fc0092da0776b4b59b9b249"},"q-b6c84c7c7c35":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Methionine","explanation":"Legumes are generally relatively low in methionine and cysteine compared with their lysine content, making sulfur amino acids limiting.","optionReasons":{"C":"Legumes are generally relatively low in methionine and cysteine compared with their lysine content, making sulfur amino acids limiting."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0ae2e7849adc2a194e4a1f85840780cf453e1853e573de5b15ae634bc9fe06fa"},"q-3cb1bdf0450b":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Isoleucine","explanation":"Isoleucine is hydrophobic and has a nonpolar branched side chain; arginine and aspartate are charged at physiological pH.","optionReasons":{"A":"Isoleucine is hydrophobic and has a nonpolar branched side chain; arginine and aspartate are charged at physiological pH."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"eedd50e29d8be6687ced76b7db1cb2470b2df3a8d1409e0b44170a57a6cb4038"},"q-d1d366a5b338":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Optically inactive","explanation":"Glycine has two hydrogen substituents on its alpha carbon, so it is achiral and optically inactive.","optionReasons":{"A":"Glycine has two hydrogen substituents on its alpha carbon, so it is achiral and optically inactive."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"d504e3ae3f9836d363594f532796ffb7c835406acec085d18a8910d884e35711"},"q-4e69d2f3316c":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Valine","explanation":"Valine has a nonpolar side chain that is preferentially buried in the hydrophobic interior of a globular protein.","optionReasons":{"B":"Valine has a nonpolar side chain that is preferentially buried in the hydrophobic interior of a globular protein."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"95c608fc2608aad6b1ed0a32910f59b0439c03af6757ae42826de0c1990c7a8f"},"q-f65c36114320":{"correct":"A","answerText":"creatine kinase","explanation":"Creatine kinase historically rises earlier after myocardial infarction than LDH and aminotransferases; modern diagnosis relies on cardiac troponin.","optionReasons":{"A":"Creatine kinase historically rises earlier after myocardial infarction than LDH and aminotransferases; modern diagnosis relies on cardiac troponin."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"4433cb0a0697f4bd75f4093c10a5053dd8ae7af92afcff12068b922af6f12035"},"q-c7d4615a306d":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Oxidative phosphorylation.","explanation":"The mitochondrial electron transport chain and ATP synthase perform oxidative phosphorylation; glycolysis occurs in the cytosol.","optionReasons":{"C":"The mitochondrial electron transport chain and ATP synthase perform oxidative phosphorylation; glycolysis occurs in the cytosol."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"b513dfd8c4502d17b5ab93e170bd4ebbcb61dbdb1466b8bd4c25e40ed8ba7308"},"q-be2ac2b893bb":{"correct":"D","answerText":"COX-1 is constitutive while COX-2 is largely inducible (p. 157-158)","explanation":"COX-1 is usually constitutively expressed, whereas COX-2 is strongly inducible at inflammatory sites, although both have physiological roles.","optionReasons":{"D":"COX-1 is usually constitutively expressed, whereas COX-2 is strongly inducible at inflammatory sites, although both have physiological roles."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"20093e36a4e8a5e8191bd9a231c5f6e87e3e11f42832644edcff63f6fa05bba8"},"q-e26eac70450a":{"correct":"B","answerText":"It is inducible","explanation":"COX-2 expression is inducible by cytokines and inflammatory stimuli; it is not confined to fetal tissues.","optionReasons":{"B":"COX-2 expression is inducible by cytokines and inflammatory stimuli; it is not confined to fetal tissues."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"400e5ce965ad8127717132032cc97ceb1f5d9b11e4f4c89dd8a01d2ea7f9bbf9"},"q-454986247014":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Theophylline (p. 191)","explanation":"Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase and affects metabolism of thiopurines; theophylline is not a xanthine-oxidase-dependent thiopurine.","optionReasons":{"D":"Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase and affects metabolism of thiopurines; theophylline is not a xanthine-oxidase-dependent thiopurine."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"37f52392299938519f83a3c10a49a59c1b3f62b50cc5ff86a51b384be231fb84"},"q-f2122c30534e":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Brain","explanation":"Brain metabolic activity is relatively less sensitive to thyroid hormone than the metabolism of heart, liver and skeletal muscle.","optionReasons":{"A":"Brain metabolic activity is relatively less sensitive to thyroid hormone than the metabolism of heart, liver and skeletal muscle."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"7dcef3bfce5bb29124a617d3219ffe9dc68820bf56dfc67f636a98ad0c3b0c76"},"q-95c637671446":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Phospholipase-A","explanation":"Glucocorticoids induce annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and reduces arachidonic acid availability for eicosanoid synthesis.","optionReasons":{"C":"Glucocorticoids induce annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and reduces arachidonic acid availability for eicosanoid synthesis."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"fa6d231a36cc72dd44add66226ca0fc2ec27449e6eede189855a5c5b1844f760"},"q-226f879008bf":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Liver","explanation":"Orally absorbed drugs enter the portal circulation and liver, where presystemic metabolism can reduce bioavailability.","optionReasons":{"D":"Orally absorbed drugs enter the portal circulation and liver, where presystemic metabolism can reduce bioavailability."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ae2088685289a2f59e2d55fa4c41e3c001f750b39b9f6cdf0f6b07da88b12ced"},"q-31c1891fd6eb":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Tyrosine","explanation":"Tyrosine is hydroxylated to DOPA and converted through dopamine and norepinephrine into epinephrine.","optionReasons":{"D":"Tyrosine is hydroxylated to DOPA and converted through dopamine and norepinephrine into epinephrine."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a9c33c1cacb767bef325be00a76769ea6779f0b5f1f7afca2d578bdf4f13f726"},"q-ec989ece8368":{"correct":"A","answerText":"acetylation","explanation":"Acetylation is a phase II conjugation reaction; deamination, hydrolysis, oxidation and reduction are typically phase I reactions.","optionReasons":{"A":"Acetylation is a phase II conjugation reaction; deamination, hydrolysis, oxidation and reduction are typically phase I reactions."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"2b3e852e9b1c5a40a91ec1b2f1cc87e21b5e256c01a4231b5b95a5141b23b0c4"},"q-a835b4bb61e1":{"correct":"A","answerText":"E. coli","explanation":"E. coli is an oxidase-negative, glucose- and lactose-fermenting Gram-negative rod and is commonly indole positive.","optionReasons":{"A":"E. coli is an oxidase-negative, glucose- and lactose-fermenting Gram-negative rod and is commonly indole positive."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"f83adbeaf29fc65ca59e863dd7abfe26644764a531048518116c0eea64c75b5f"},"q-d6d2ae1946d7":{"correct":"D","answerText":"amikacin","explanation":"Amikacin was designed to resist many aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes and often remains active against organisms resistant to older drugs.","optionReasons":{"D":"Amikacin was designed to resist many aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes and often remains active against organisms resistant to older drugs."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"8c1e1d8dfdf95508700388186af06082bfcbd0314cc087df5cca661f2eace194"},"q-06f2aba408a9":{"correct":"A","answerText":"keto acid","explanation":"Transamination transfers an amino group to an alpha-keto acid to create another amino acid.","optionReasons":{"A":"Transamination transfers an amino group to an alpha-keto acid to create another amino acid."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5155226a1062415fedcb226ffce757dc91bba0941603b42fac3465aa46742a02"},"q-90b13a446839":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Gluconeogenesis","explanation":"Gluconeogenesis synthesizes glucose from noncarbohydrate precursors such as lactate, glycerol and glucogenic amino acids.","optionReasons":{"D":"Gluconeogenesis synthesizes glucose from noncarbohydrate precursors such as lactate, glycerol and glucogenic amino acids."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"feb16721ab6d04259e9f61304eca876909f36a8e6863fd489fd1f078eebec18d"},"q-7f91212154e0":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Set free","explanation":"An enzyme acts as a catalyst and is released in a usable form after product formation instead of being consumed stoichiometrically.","optionReasons":{"B":"An enzyme acts as a catalyst and is released in a usable form after product formation instead of being consumed stoichiometrically."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5a627de222e442988dadacc67d5602bbad1bd92e0c119e0423d7ec8686ffc42f"},"q-4e0ab9bd12c6":{"correct":"B","answerText":"hydrophobic","explanation":"Phospholipid fatty acid tails are hydrophobic and orient inward within the membrane, away from water.","optionReasons":{"B":"Phospholipid fatty acid tails are hydrophobic and orient inward within the membrane, away from water."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"7b8c334fb42119e586a3eef7006382e037433e8921f016fbac8b0dc8a38a2eb5"},"q-ec3ed497ab37":{"correct":"A","answerText":"lower activation energy","explanation":"Enzymes catalyze reactions by lowering activation energy without changing the equilibrium constant.","optionReasons":{"A":"Enzymes catalyze reactions by lowering activation energy without changing the equilibrium constant."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"326cd5c73135ffe2fa390f2e84e9fe314529d40eb792dea44b3188abefea7684"},"q-9add09900dcc":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Changing the conformation of the enzyme by binding to site other than catalytic site","explanation":"Noncompetitive inhibitors often bind an allosteric site and lower catalytic efficiency by changing enzyme conformation.","optionReasons":{"C":"Noncompetitive inhibitors often bind an allosteric site and lower catalytic efficiency by changing enzyme conformation."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"da12ededcf2b3d26f504befcd304c9924b47501e8d10ae2b6e919c1d6e35c953"},"q-b1ff881c895b":{"correct":"D","answerText":"amino transferance","explanation":"Enzymes transferring amino groups are called aminotransferases or transaminases, including ALT and AST.","optionReasons":{"D":"Enzymes transferring amino groups are called aminotransferases or transaminases, including ALT and AST."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a2e59fd8e0c9813d50c85c555dd4d095774c6b8598a8a1fc279faad139360768"},"q-717cd31f8147":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Zwitterions","explanation":"Zwitterions carry both positive and negative functional-group charges within one molecule while having zero net charge at the isoelectric point.","optionReasons":{"C":"Zwitterions carry both positive and negative functional-group charges within one molecule while having zero net charge at the isoelectric point."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"2727fc680a5f177a13e0d22621002b464c1e78bd0df140d9f0170cc11d2c2658"},"q-d46e535efd7f":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Enolase.","explanation":"Fluoride inhibits enolase in glycolysis, which is why fluoride-containing tubes can help reduce glycolytic glucose consumption.","optionReasons":{"B":"Fluoride inhibits enolase in glycolysis, which is why fluoride-containing tubes can help reduce glycolytic glucose consumption."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"85716d2b5f72d5825bdc19562bf7c14cf956f5ec8ebfdb3e3a13af61446bb9ba"},"q-445ba54a0598":{"correct":"D","answerText":"ATP.","explanation":"Anaerobic glycolysis converts glucose to lactate and produces a net yield of two ATP per glucose; NAD+ is regenerated during lactate formation.","optionReasons":{"D":"Anaerobic glycolysis converts glucose to lactate and produces a net yield of two ATP per glucose; NAD+ is regenerated during lactate formation."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"03adfc5e50a5e962d51c6025de901ad8b25c66a947ccefe7c6aeed61fb4b0237"},"q-185d5917408b":{"correct":"A","answerText":"The clavicle ossifies in the foetus.","explanation":"The clavicle begins ossification early in fetal life and is among the first bones to ossify; the acromion, not coracoid, continues laterally from the scapular spine.","optionReasons":{"A":"The clavicle begins ossification early in fetal life and is among the first bones to ossify; the acromion, not coracoid, continues laterally from the scapular spine."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"24f090c3020f97591bba4943ccd494cdcd6af8fe359e25fe6ec10c8163311c68"},"q-a982144edb57":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Pectoralis major and minor contribute to the ante- rior wall.","explanation":"Pectoralis major and pectoralis minor constitute much of the anterior axillary wall together with associated fascia.","optionReasons":{"A":"Pectoralis major and pectoralis minor constitute much of the anterior axillary wall together with associated fascia."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"efa579e603968f75d62a9527da8bf92bb21c68048262b325b64fd7c10813c798"},"q-ba5339f4da40":{"correct":"D","answerText":"The action of the lumbricals is to extend the dis- tal interphalangeal joint.","explanation":"The lumbricals join digital extensor expansions to extend interphalangeal joints while flexing metacarpophalangeal joints; they arise from profundus tendons.","optionReasons":{"D":"The lumbricals join digital extensor expansions to extend interphalangeal joints while flexing metacarpophalangeal joints; they arise from profundus tendons."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"b40b72a5bacb98eed90df03d98f18a8c5223b7f6ae1934b0efe6d3d912aa7204"},"q-639ed5a4a248":{"correct":"E","answerText":"It is prone to aneurysmal change in the presence of a cervical rib. 7","explanation":"A cervical rib can compress the subclavian artery, producing post-stenotic dilatation or aneurysmal change.","optionReasons":{"E":"A cervical rib can compress the subclavian artery, producing post-stenotic dilatation or aneurysmal change."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ac187f38779f606188361971c684941988005a3be73ae3c43c23f9eae06de686"},"q-71afcedf801b":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Gives no muscular branches in the arm.","explanation":"The median nerve usually gives no muscular branches in the arm; in the forearm it enters between the two heads of pronator teres.","optionReasons":{"C":"The median nerve usually gives no muscular branches in the arm; in the forearm it enters between the two heads of pronator teres."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"676b551c8c296b7751bd2a87a13922136bb904f5b2019bb4f0d5049902ca9170"},"q-aa2b78b524a9":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Deltoid may atrophy following shoulder dislocation. 9","explanation":"Anterior-inferior shoulder dislocation can injure the axillary nerve as it encircles the surgical neck of the humerus, causing deltoid atrophy.","optionReasons":{"E":"Anterior-inferior shoulder dislocation can injure the axillary nerve as it encircles the surgical neck of the humerus, causing deltoid atrophy."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"96cab05e82d5c20b36c138c11edc411162006b5e4d866e2af1a4526b6b0b52be"},"q-0602dde71b29":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Clavicle derives its cutaneous innervation from C4.","explanation":"The supraclavicular nerves supplying skin over the clavicle predominantly arise from C3–C4 cervical spinal roots.","optionReasons":{"A":"The supraclavicular nerves supplying skin over the clavicle predominantly arise from C3–C4 cervical spinal roots."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a04fdd979afc7c0f7678444131ebfbad52c02c12659ca7fdf74928eed1586a66"},"q-263c7afc0920":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Biceps jerk is C5.","explanation":"The biceps tendon reflex assesses predominantly C5, with contribution from C6 via the musculocutaneous nerve.","optionReasons":{"A":"The biceps tendon reflex assesses predominantly C5, with contribution from C6 via the musculocutaneous nerve."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1d14cb262bd05fe4acb09b64280fd5fd2e6f7dd498191a60ac95a196f9d7da34"},"q-2ab680b837ff":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Gluteus minimus is attached to the lesser trochanter.","explanation":"Gluteus minimus inserts onto the greater trochanter rather than the lesser trochanter, which receives the iliopsoas tendon.","optionReasons":{"C":"Gluteus minimus inserts onto the greater trochanter rather than the lesser trochanter, which receives the iliopsoas tendon."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a0ac05efc6edf60c5d7298166640ec83482fb9c89f8d0ec7b8af0288e3581983"},"q-cf580c7b3c79":{"correct":"D","answerText":"There are three compartments.","explanation":"The thigh is classically divided into three compartments: anterior extensors, medial adductors and posterior flexors.","optionReasons":{"D":"The thigh is classically divided into three compartments: anterior extensors, medial adductors and posterior flexors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"196ac2d4550abae1ee901c01edb3d562d728e128e0b1b34e880a2982b4fe8bb6"},"q-13fdcf2530b1":{"correct":"C","answerText":"The quadriceps tendon inserts directly onto the tibia.","explanation":"The quadriceps tendon inserts on the patella; the patellar ligament then connects the patella to the tibial tuberosity, so insertion is not direct.","optionReasons":{"C":"The quadriceps tendon inserts on the patella; the patellar ligament then connects the patella to the tibial tuberosity, so insertion is not direct."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"f25ec274896e8542c5c095fdfdcb98a4563c689fdee9924e4a2af96025c1ccf2"},"q-acac5ec3424f":{"correct":"E","answerText":"The femoral artery exits through a hiatus in adductor longus. 30","explanation":"The femoral artery traverses the adductor hiatus in adductor magnus, not adductor longus, to become the popliteal artery.","optionReasons":{"E":"The femoral artery traverses the adductor hiatus in adductor magnus, not adductor longus, to become the popliteal artery."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1452f9d6bd13aa90197957da820466fd3fa6600eac1d7bc69a78598da0b05607"},"q-f0e9f5a6c807":{"correct":"B","answerText":"The suprapatellar bursa communicates with the knee joint.","explanation":"The suprapatellar bursa is ordinarily continuous with the knee synovial cavity, allowing communication during joint effusion.","optionReasons":{"B":"The suprapatellar bursa is ordinarily continuous with the knee synovial cavity, allowing communication during joint effusion."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"3e02a91d3c7f28f50faf39d24a852ac7ab0a89dcbb8c153a7895b079c5b5be59"},"q-7f9c760973c4":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Contains the long saphenous vein in its roof.","explanation":"The great (long) saphenous vein lies medially and does not traverse the popliteal fossa roof; the small saphenous vein does.","optionReasons":{"D":"The great (long) saphenous vein lies medially and does not traverse the popliteal fossa roof; the small saphenous vein does."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5c975d4f64c491ffc57d020c885a5ac4e8dad9453b51a289b61dec38bd61ed47"},"q-f9b856853ca9":{"correct":"B","answerText":"All the muscles of the compartment are innerv- ated by the deep peroneal nerve.","explanation":"Anterior leg compartment muscles are supplied by the deep fibular (peroneal) nerve and are principally involved in dorsiflexion.","optionReasons":{"B":"Anterior leg compartment muscles are supplied by the deep fibular (peroneal) nerve and are principally involved in dorsiflexion."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"055bbf68a560c97e4db6ea587d3d6a66b01364d125cf79d89f8391f9bf2e831f"},"q-c8ad4e69326b":{"correct":"C","answerText":"The muscles of facial expression are derived from the second arch.","explanation":"Second pharyngeal arch mesenchyme forms muscles of facial expression supplied by the facial nerve (CN VII).","optionReasons":{"C":"Second pharyngeal arch mesenchyme forms muscles of facial expression supplied by the facial nerve (CN VII)."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"1a2f36c717e6e0dd65a704afc6dc76ad12a88291597150db38558872e3b4f787"},"q-44a3e16cc752":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Closure of the eye is controlled by the facial nerve.","explanation":"Orbicularis oculi, the eyelid-closing muscle, is supplied by the facial nerve; most extraocular muscles are supplied by III, IV or VI.","optionReasons":{"E":"Orbicularis oculi, the eyelid-closing muscle, is supplied by the facial nerve; most extraocular muscles are supplied by III, IV or VI."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"0e9f2282ebad1c52ebe9b53e2059912d5d410a98cc85af45f5027ffc0215bedd"},"q-1ff3ff0d1ffd":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Is drained from its most dependent part.","explanation":"The maxillary sinus drains high on its medial wall into the middle meatus; its drainage opening is not at the most dependent point.","optionReasons":{"C":"The maxillary sinus drains high on its medial wall into the middle meatus; its drainage opening is not at the most dependent point."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"08f6180e4eaa5c4ad687da93495fe95d999db1370d909d2e3903fc7014cbf278"},"q-16192a8a9098":{"correct":"B","answerText":"They form six distinct cavities.","explanation":"The ethmoid labyrinth contains multiple variable air cells, not a fixed set of six; anterior cells drain to the middle meatus and posterior cells to the superior meatus.","optionReasons":{"B":"The ethmoid labyrinth contains multiple variable air cells, not a fixed set of six; anterior cells drain to the middle meatus and posterior cells to the superior meatus."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"906d48108b9c14c63390545db706557c57e78f018e3acc09d53af4ebb96ad851"},"q-a877addef96f":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Receives its blood supply from a direct branch of the external carotid artery.","explanation":"The tongue receives its main arterial supply from the lingual artery, a direct branch of the external carotid artery.","optionReasons":{"B":"The tongue receives its main arterial supply from the lingual artery, a direct branch of the external carotid artery."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"c5543439c6efd77389605d877172bc748a93085815da42a7f6e110e7e6392bf3"},"q-c66e203c3b21":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Contains the external jugular vein.","explanation":"The external jugular vein usually crosses superficially over sternocleidomastoid in the posterior triangle rather than being a structure of the anterior triangle.","optionReasons":{"D":"The external jugular vein usually crosses superficially over sternocleidomastoid in the posterior triangle rather than being a structure of the anterior triangle."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"d1e7a20ae486397b4c45913634d1ae3474299c848fdab21bd2b868ec5a1a64b0"},"q-fed2d0f94ead":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Simulium(For Oncocerca volvulus)","explanation":"Female Simulium blackflies transmit Onchocerca volvulus during blood feeding, whereas the other listed arthropods are not its vectors.","optionReasons":{"A":"Female Simulium blackflies transmit Onchocerca volvulus during blood feeding, whereas the other listed arthropods are not its vectors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"94d615e53ce94bf516c46e9fb024017b5b69eeba0386c6b09ce81080473a7d77"},"q-91c0d7e4561a":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Bubonic Plague","explanation":"Plague caused by Yersinia pestis can persist in US wildlife reservoirs and is transmitted by infected fleas.","optionReasons":{"C":"Plague caused by Yersinia pestis can persist in US wildlife reservoirs and is transmitted by infected fleas."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"db976ef94632bdfc99f17b881d1211d868c706c30f357d131b68d8cd98a33cb0"},"q-ee1649714587":{"correct":"B","answerText":"pedipalps","explanation":"Pedipalps are appendages of arachnids such as spiders and scorpions, not of six-legged insects.","optionReasons":{"B":"Pedipalps are appendages of arachnids such as spiders and scorpions, not of six-legged insects."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"bc093ea461fb61dadfdd9961203cef88cce9808881ec541431999a9651c68b53"},"q-3e7fa75f3279":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Triatoma","explanation":"Triatomine kissing bugs retain fully developed hemipteran wings, whereas lice, fleas and bedbugs are wingless or secondarily reduced.","optionReasons":{"B":"Triatomine kissing bugs retain fully developed hemipteran wings, whereas lice, fleas and bedbugs are wingless or secondarily reduced."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"85e840656af4f279ef8ef2e7c207408f8b078cfe79fb22dd609ff46849695423"},"q-8512a419b165":{"correct":"C","answerText":"metastigmata","explanation":"Hard and soft ticks have historically been grouped in the metastigmatid lineage of acarines, unlike mesostigmatid mites.","optionReasons":{"C":"Hard and soft ticks have historically been grouped in the metastigmatid lineage of acarines, unlike mesostigmatid mites."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"b2fe87b363b0470f6ee7aa0f2ac02276d67170252313f36ba1b2f2819b4ed17f"},"q-84da8361c790":{"correct":"D","answerText":"the presence of head lice indicates that the person has chronically poor hygiene","explanation":"Head lice spread through close head contact and are not a reliable marker of poor personal hygiene; infestations occur in clean and unclean hair.","optionReasons":{"D":"Head lice spread through close head contact and are not a reliable marker of poor personal hygiene; infestations occur in clean and unclean hair."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"7befff6c4c6386e343ca483cf9b90160385574e1e84bc1e786331ab934ccccad"},"q-2e31b0ddb13f":{"correct":"D","answerText":"methane (intestinal gas)","explanation":"Human-attracted mosquitoes respond to carbon dioxide, body odors, warmth, movement and visual cues; intestinal methane is not a recognized major human-attraction cue.","optionReasons":{"D":"Human-attracted mosquitoes respond to carbon dioxide, body odors, warmth, movement and visual cues; intestinal methane is not a recognized major human-attraction cue."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"fbb1a8ca9169edba324d4758295de70dbcfe12f8b1ce1e5ae1539e410d758b05"},"q-a8dc4d5626cf":{"correct":"C","answerText":"jointed feet","explanation":"Arthropoda comes from Greek roots for jointed and foot, reflecting the phylum's jointed appendages.","optionReasons":{"C":"Arthropoda comes from Greek roots for jointed and foot, reflecting the phylum's jointed appendages."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"ece1b99f3b33b0521008edcc19e82608a66cb8b878bb7f67033de2633adc2588"},"q-70c24f4b76b3":{"correct":"B","answerText":"pedipalps","explanation":"Pedipalps occur in arachnids, not insects; prothorax, spiracles and uniramous appendages are insect-associated structures.","optionReasons":{"B":"Pedipalps occur in arachnids, not insects; prothorax, spiracles and uniramous appendages are insect-associated structures."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5c8eb201597f326e5e812baaf295a4efacdf3ab7b2c238b713a4bdfde554f229"},"q-16bc0ffb648e":{"correct":"C","answerText":"birds","explanation":"Chewing or biting lice in the Mallophaga group are especially common ectoparasites of birds.","optionReasons":{"C":"Chewing or biting lice in the Mallophaga group are especially common ectoparasites of birds."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"aa4eec54635b341cfbae54d4ec4c39563821f404d8a4f6108ded30e1f2a08079"},"q-29b925e7d69e":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Class Crustacea","explanation":"Argulus fish lice are crustacean branchiurans; despite their common name, they are not insects or true lice.","optionReasons":{"D":"Argulus fish lice are crustacean branchiurans; despite their common name, they are not insects or true lice."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"a45adb0ea3526504d641b171b707766c73d46fa40996c0a3455e927a0f0b4e7d"},"q-82a93af4af5f":{"correct":"D","answerText":"pull it out by grasping its anterior end with a tweezers and applying steady pressure","explanation":"Remove an attached tick with fine-tipped forceps grasping close to the skin and pulling steadily; burning or applying chemicals can cause injury.","optionReasons":{"D":"Remove an attached tick with fine-tipped forceps grasping close to the skin and pulling steadily; burning or applying chemicals can cause injury."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"9a8bdd85c5550b29a949fa76c10351909a6c82320d42618b9747e639b4fa2df5"},"q-4b6b094eb5c2":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Gastrophilis intestinalis","explanation":"Gasterophilus adults do not have functional piercing blood-feeding mouthparts; mosquitoes, tsetse and blackflies have adapted biting structures.","optionReasons":{"A":"Gasterophilus adults do not have functional piercing blood-feeding mouthparts; mosquitoes, tsetse and blackflies have adapted biting structures."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"bbfd29e4dcc68b63816355c5ab9be569209eaee35c414b73c4d203be74fd5eb7"},"q-ce0add1a706c":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Demodex","explanation":"Demodex mites dwell in hair follicles and are commonly sampled by skin scraping or related follicular collection methods.","optionReasons":{"E":"Demodex mites dwell in hair follicles and are commonly sampled by skin scraping or related follicular collection methods."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"72285b2f9e73dbe2ad6b49c6ce1cf19d3f5be75cae293dc4d1484326a3bf60dd"},"q-409eef0039bd":{"correct":"C","answerText":"definitive host","explanation":"The definitive host carries the sexually mature, reproductive phase of a parasite; intermediate hosts carry other developmental stages.","optionReasons":{"C":"The definitive host carries the sexually mature, reproductive phase of a parasite; intermediate hosts carry other developmental stages."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"f9a616fed436e8b155e10f3207859c5372cb996fbc3f03f1b44f0602ca789e5f"},"q-622e836e41c1":{"correct":"A","answerText":"polluted water","explanation":"Culex quinquefasciatus commonly breeds in organically polluted standing water, including poorly drained urban sites.","optionReasons":{"A":"Culex quinquefasciatus commonly breeds in organically polluted standing water, including poorly drained urban sites."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"58a78f379ffebe71e15e44e1f3b4f44fefa804a8bf52eac27b69e279db4e4825"},"q-3ad864ab792d":{"correct":"C","answerText":"An. arabiensis","explanation":"Anopheles arabiensis thrives across drier ecological zones and can contribute to seasonal and epidemic malaria transmission.","optionReasons":{"C":"Anopheles arabiensis thrives across drier ecological zones and can contribute to seasonal and epidemic malaria transmission."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"01f643ec6e889a6296b4755799ce64b76ada21c4e9b763f6792e67aadd6402f9"},"q-97efbc6ca384":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Meloidae","explanation":"Blister beetles of the family Meloidae can contain cantharidin, a vesicant that causes skin blistering on contact.","optionReasons":{"E":"Blister beetles of the family Meloidae can contain cantharidin, a vesicant that causes skin blistering on contact."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"e96f60717a9c9a893d5691b3adcf7645cb035440056dd67a5c3489d3eca3c51d"},"q-c2f8ef5099cf":{"correct":"D","answerText":"complete digestive system (mouth and anus) Hermaphroditism","explanation":"Flatworms are acoelomate and generally lack a complete alimentary tract with separate mouth and anus; cestodes lack a gut entirely.","optionReasons":{"D":"Flatworms are acoelomate and generally lack a complete alimentary tract with separate mouth and anus; cestodes lack a gut entirely."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"00a223d2ef9993b5f83015eabd120af5e85faded0f1a76bb798f57d9240719e6"},"q-f1be969b5c80":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Leishmania spp","explanation":"Phlebotomine sandflies transmit Leishmania protozoa during feeding; malaria, Loa and Onchocerca have different insect vectors.","optionReasons":{"B":"Phlebotomine sandflies transmit Leishmania protozoa during feeding; malaria, Loa and Onchocerca have different insect vectors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"008a0b7dbb0cacf0aab46bea14b13a5eb4d92ca78851225bf3aec6d1de6fab89"},"q-575db0b09a91":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Leishmania spp","explanation":"New World Lutzomyia sandflies transmit Leishmania species; tsetse and reduviid bugs transmit other trypanosomes.","optionReasons":{"D":"New World Lutzomyia sandflies transmit Leishmania species; tsetse and reduviid bugs transmit other trypanosomes."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"e2fcedca76f200cec903a4a9863f50ec30ae46101b516ee72e2623f98255d36c"},"q-55f813815f08":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Giardia lamblia","explanation":"Giardia is a flagellated protozoan, while Ascaris, Trichuris and Strongyloides are nematode worms.","optionReasons":{"D":"Giardia is a flagellated protozoan, while Ascaris, Trichuris and Strongyloides are nematode worms."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"50d5f40b1cdac3147e392bed1c580e94612462485e457a53e8952c0c13b5d8ee"},"q-1e57f9727385":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Pork Tapeworm","explanation":"Taenia solium is the pork tapeworm; T. saginata is associated with beef.","optionReasons":{"C":"Taenia solium is the pork tapeworm; T. saginata is associated with beef."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"f4402740c3572ddffa7258d8657d8c4e1c806689d246f3424e4e5a3dda7fa30f"},"q-8daae0570ad4":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Ingestion of cysticercus larvae by human in undercooked or raw meat","explanation":"Human taeniasis with T. saginata follows ingestion of viable cysticerci in undercooked infected beef, not ingestion of eggs.","optionReasons":{"D":"Human taeniasis with T. saginata follows ingestion of viable cysticerci in undercooked infected beef, not ingestion of eggs."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"72d582fb505fc5d8fdc2299dc494bbb4fd0425ba8f1f5a855a8ecc8605808783"},"q-e4d58f07fd4b":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Trichinella spiralis.","explanation":"Trichinella infection is acquired from eating encysted larvae in meat rather than classical soil-mediated transmission.","optionReasons":{"D":"Trichinella infection is acquired from eating encysted larvae in meat rather than classical soil-mediated transmission."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"399e7700b6ad152bd429343b20e9174580cb1612534fb8d4d2f5b3b47c4db5fa"},"q-bbd9518b71af":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Plasmodium knowlesi","explanation":"Plasmodium knowlesi is a zoonotic malaria parasite maintained primarily in macaque reservoirs and transmitted by Anopheles vectors.","optionReasons":{"B":"Plasmodium knowlesi is a zoonotic malaria parasite maintained primarily in macaque reservoirs and transmitted by Anopheles vectors."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"414cc03d94c2ec4e375a5f45e207373d76e171a6abe105e35b172e53a9de2586"},"q-01a309be3ad0":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Reservoir host in which asexual development of the parasite","explanation":"Humans support asexual Plasmodium stages and are the intermediate host; sexual development occurs in the female Anopheles mosquito, the definitive host.","optionReasons":{"A":"Humans support asexual Plasmodium stages and are the intermediate host; sexual development occurs in the female Anopheles mosquito, the definitive host."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"5d3d0abba4ad89f3bdabc3806b49d70b0272842bcc594e04335a9d7561518d02"},"q-0379147d02b5":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Not exceeding 10% in children aged 2-9 years","explanation":"Under the older spleen-rate classification, hypoendemic malaria has a spleen rate below or equal to 10% in children aged 2–9 years.","optionReasons":{"A":"Under the older spleen-rate classification, hypoendemic malaria has a spleen rate below or equal to 10% in children aged 2–9 years."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash":"41548e0e49915eeca0aa7c9ccca0bc6262591dc3e15893fc429756596d313019"},"q-0e40b5f4385c":{"correct":"C","answerText":"coenurus","explanation":"Taenia multiceps larvae develop as coenuri, cyst-like forms containing multiple protoscolices.","optionReasons":{"C":"Taenia multiceps larvae develop as coenuri, cyst-like forms containing multiple protoscolices."},"references":[],"review":"new_curated_independent_reasoning_without_external_verification","reviewDate":"2026-10-20","provisional":true,"sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"questionTextHash